Kept this version close to the problem statement. The first loop picks one number, and the second loop checks every number after it. Starting the second loop at `i + 1` avoids using the same position twice and avoids checking a pair again.

For example, with `[2, 7, 11, 15]` and target `9`, the pair at indices `0` and `1` is found immediately. This uses no extra data structure, but it can be slow when the array is large.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++i) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++j) {
                if (nums[i] + nums[j] == target) {
                    return {i, j};
                }
            }
        }
        return {};
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because all possible pairs may be checked.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The input array is not changed.

This version fills the map first, then searches for each number's complement. Keeping the two passes separate makes the different-index check easy to see. If the value appears twice, the stored index must not equal the current index.

The problem normally guarantees a pair, but returning an empty array keeps the method safe for an input without one. No sorting is needed, so the original order and indices stay the same.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> indexByValue = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            indexByValue.put(nums[i], i);
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int needed = target - nums[i];
            if (indexByValue.containsKey(needed)
                    && indexByValue.get(needed) != i) {
                return new int[] {i, indexByValue.get(needed)};
            }
        }

        return new int[] {};
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` for the two array passes, with average constant-time map operations.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the value-to-index map.

A different option is to sort pairs containing both the number and its original index. After sorting, one pointer starts at the smallest value and another at the largest value. If their sum is too small, move the left pointer. If it is too large, move the right pointer.

The original indices are kept inside each pair, so sorting does not lose the answer positions. The trade-off is that sorting takes more time than a one-pass hash map.

Edge cases such as negative values and duplicate values work because the pair list keeps every input position separately.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        vector<pair<int, int>> values;
        values.reserve(nums.size());

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++i) {
            values.push_back({nums[i], i});
        }

        sort(values.begin(), values.end());

        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(values.size()) - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            long long sum = static_cast<long long>(values[left].first)
                          + values[right].first;

            if (sum == target) {
                return {values[left].second, values[right].second};
            }
            if (sum < target) {
                ++left;
            } else {
                --right;
            }
        }

        return {};
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because the value-index pairs are sorted.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the copied pairs. The input array is not modified.

Here the first pass records which index can provide the complement of each number. The second pass looks at the actual values and checks whether the current value is one of those complements. The index comparison prevents a number from matching with itself.

For `nums = [3, 2, 4]` and target `6`, the first pass records that `3` needs index `0`, `4` needs index `1`, and `2` needs index `2`. During the scan, value `2` matches the stored complement for index `2`, giving the pair `[1, 2]`.

An empty list is returned if no valid pair exists, even though the usual input guarantees one.

```python
class Solution:
    def twoSum(self, nums, target):
        complement_index = {}

        for index, number in enumerate(nums):
            complement_index[target - number] = index

        for index, number in enumerate(nums):
            if number in complement_index:
                other_index = complement_index[number]
                if other_index != index:
                    return [index, other_index]

        return []
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average, using two linear scans.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the complement dictionary.

## Intuition
The brute-force solution checks every possible pair with nested loops in $O(n^2)$ time. Instead of repeatedly scanning the array, we can use a hash map to keep track of elements we have already seen alongside their indices. For each element $x$, the required pairing value is fixed: $\text{complement} = \text{target} - x$. If that complement is already present in our map, we can return the answer immediately in a single pass.

## Approach
1. Initialize an empty hash map `indexMap` to store each number and its index: `(nums[i] -> i)`.
2. Iterate through the array with index `i`:
   - Compute `complement = target - nums[i]`.
   - Check if `complement` exists in `indexMap`.
   - If it exists, return a new array containing `i` and `indexMap.get(complement)`.
   - If not, insert `nums[i]` and `i` into `indexMap` so subsequent elements can pair with it.
3. Return an empty array if no pair satisfies the condition.

## Solution
```java[]
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> indexMap = new HashMap<>();
        int n = nums.length;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int complement = target - nums[i];
            if (indexMap.containsKey(complement)) {
                return new int[]{i, indexMap.get(complement)};
            }
            indexMap.put(nums[i], i);
        }

        return new int[]{};
    }
}
```

## Complexity
- **Time Complexity:**
  $O(n)$, where $n$ is the length of `nums`. The array is traversed only once, and hash map operations (`containsKey` and `put`) run in average $O(1)$ time.

- **Space Complexity:**
  $O(n)$, because the hash map stores at most $n$ key-value pairs in the worst case before finding the matching complement.

1. Store a mapping of array elements and their index in hashmap
2. When you iterate the array, first check if a corresponding target-nums[i] element exists. If it exists, you have found your answer. 
3. If not, keep inserting the array elements with indices.

